# Pediatric Cardiology (Baker / Foster slides) — Version 2 (image quiz), 18 items, every stem has an image
Q = [
dict(img='Pkdface', cap="Photograph of the child's face",
stem="A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of fever up to 39.8°C (103.6°F) for 6 days despite acetaminophen. He has been irritable and has a rash on his trunk. Examination shows bilateral conjunctival injection without discharge, a 2-cm tender anterior cervical lymph node, and red, swollen hands and feet. His face is shown.{FIG}In addition to an echocardiogram, which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
choices=["Immune globulin plus aspirin","Intramuscular benzathine penicillin","Oral amoxicillin","High-dose prednisone alone","Ibuprofen and observation"], key=0,
exp="This is <b>Kawasaki disease</b>: fever for 5 or more days plus red, dry, cracked lips (shown), nonexudative conjunctivitis, a cervical node, a rash, and red, swollen hands and feet. Dr. Baker's rule: <b>a child diagnosed with Kawasaki disease needs an urgent echocardiogram</b> to look for <b>coronary artery aneurysms</b>, and <b>aspirin and intravenous immune globulin (IVIG) start immediately</b>. His residency paper showed IVIG shrinking existing aneurysms. Board add-on: IVIG given within the first 10 days cuts the aneurysm rate from about 25% to about 5%, and Kawasaki disease is the exception to the rule against aspirin in children.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Kawasaki disease is treated with intravenous immune globulin plus aspirin and an urgent echocardiogram, to prevent and detect coronary artery aneurysms.",
wrong={1:"Benzathine penicillin treats streptococcal pharyngitis and prevents rheumatic fever. Antibiotics don't treat Kawasaki disease, and nonexudative conjunctivitis with red cracked lips isn't strep.",
2:"Amoxicillin treats bacterial pharyngitis and otitis. Antibiotics do nothing for the vasculitis that threatens the coronary arteries.",
3:"Steroids are at most an add-on for high-risk or IVIG-resistant cases (board add-on). Prednisone alone is not first-line treatment.",
4:"Supportive care misses the window to prevent coronary aneurysms. Ibuprofen also blunts aspirin's antiplatelet effect (board add-on)."},
eli5="Kawasaki disease is an inflammation of blood vessels that can balloon the heart's own arteries. A drip of antibodies (IVIG) plus aspirin calms it down and protects those arteries, and an ultrasound of the heart checks them."),

dict(img='Pboot', cap='Chest radiograph',
stem="A 3-month-old boy has episodes of turning blue around the lips during feeding and crying; the episodes resolve when he calms. His oxygen saturation at rest is 86%. A harsh grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border. A chest x-ray is shown.{FIG}The severity of this infant's cyanosis is determined mainly by which of the following?",
choices=["Severity of pulmonary outflow stenosis","Size of the ventricular septal defect","Thickness of the right ventricular wall","Extent of aortic override","Flow across a patent foramen ovale"], key=0,
exp="Cyanotic spells, a harsh murmur at the left upper sternal border, and a <b>boot-shaped heart</b> (an upturned apex from right ventricular hypertrophy) mean <b>tetralogy of Fallot</b>. The four defects are a large VSD, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy and an overriding aorta. The VSD is large, so both ventricles share one pressure. How much deoxygenated blood goes right-to-left into the aorta depends on how hard it is for blood to leave the right ventricle toward the lungs: the <b>degree of pulmonary (right ventricular outflow) stenosis</b>. That is why the lecture says repair is timed by the degree of stenosis. Crying raises the shunt and produces the tet spell. The murmur comes from the outflow stenosis, not the VSD.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In tetralogy of Fallot, the degree of right ventricular outflow (pulmonary) obstruction determines how much blood shunts right-to-left, and so how cyanotic the child is.",
wrong={1:"The VSD in tetralogy is large and unrestrictive in almost every case. Its size doesn't vary enough to set the shunt. Outflow resistance does.",
2:"Right ventricular hypertrophy is a <b>consequence</b> of the outflow obstruction, not what drives the shunt.",
3:"The overriding aorta receives the mixed blood, but the amount of right-to-left flow is set by the outflow obstruction.",
4:"Atrial-level shunting isn't one of the four defects and doesn't explain the cyanosis."},
eli5="In tetralogy, there's a big hole between the pumping chambers and a tight door to the lungs. The tighter that door, the more blue blood slips through the hole into the body instead of going to the lungs."),

dict(img='Pcyan', cap='Photograph of the newborn',
stem="A 3.4-kg (7.5-lb) boy is cyanotic 2 hours after an uncomplicated term delivery. His mother has type 1 diabetes mellitus. Oxygen saturation is 72% and does not rise with 100% oxygen. He is breathing comfortably and his lungs are clear. There is a single loud second heart sound and no murmur. A chest x-ray shows a narrow upper mediastinum with an egg-shaped heart. His face is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy?",
choices=["Prostaglandin E1 infusion","Intravenous indomethacin bolus","Intravenous furosemide","Inhaled nitric oxide","Oral propranolol"], key=0,
exp="Profound cyanosis in the first hours, no rise with 100% oxygen (a <b>cardiac</b> rather than lung cause), clear lungs, a single loud S2 and an \"egg on a string\" heart suggest <b>transposition of the great arteries</b>. The aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left, giving two parallel circuits. As the lecture puts it, shunts are necessary for survival: mixing must occur through a <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b>, a patent foramen ovale or a VSD. The first drug keeps the ductus open: <b>prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil)</b>. Board add-on: then a balloon atrial septostomy and the arterial switch operation. Maternal diabetes is a known risk factor.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In ductal-dependent cyanotic heart disease such as transposition, start prostaglandin E1 immediately to keep the ductus arteriosus open for mixing.",
wrong={1:"Indomethacin <b>closes</b> the ductus. In a ductal-dependent lesion that removes the mixing site and can be fatal.",
2:"There is no congestion: the lungs are clear and the infant is comfortable. A diuretic doesn't fix the mixing problem.",
3:"Nitric oxide treats persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, a lung-vessel problem. The single S2, egg-shaped heart and lack of response to oxygen point to a structural lesion.",
4:"Propranolol is used to prevent tet spells in tetralogy of Fallot, not for transposition."},
eli5="In transposition the two big arteries are swapped, so blue blood keeps going back to the body. Babies survive only if there's a connection that lets red and blue blood mix. Prostaglandin keeps one of those connections, the ductus, open."),

dict(img='Pcoarc', cap='Magnetic resonance angiogram, sagittal view of the thoracic aorta',
stem="A 14-year-old girl is evaluated for short stature and absent breast development. She has a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, a low posterior hairline, and a short, webbed neck. Blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg in the right arm, and her femoral pulses are weak and delayed. A magnetic resonance angiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following karyotypes is most likely?",
choices=["45,X","46,XX","46,XY","47,XXY","47,XX,+21"], key=0,
exp="The angiogram shows a discrete narrowing of the descending aorta (arrow) just beyond the arch, near where the ductus inserted. That is <b>coarctation of the aorta</b>, explaining the arm hypertension and weak, delayed femoral pulses. With short stature, primary amenorrhea (absent puberty), a webbed neck, low hairline and broad chest, this is <b>Turner syndrome, 45,X</b>. The lecture: about <b>25–30% of girls with Turner syndrome have coarctation</b>, and nearly as many have a <b>bicuspid aortic valve</b>, so any child with suspected Turner syndrome should get an echocardiogram.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Turner syndrome (45,X) is associated with coarctation of the aorta and bicuspid aortic valve. Screen with echocardiography.",
wrong={1:"46,XX is a normal female karyotype. Noonan syndrome (often 46,XX or 46,XY) can look similar but typically has pulmonary stenosis, not coarctation (board add-on).",
2:"46,XY with a female phenotype (androgen insensitivity) gives breast development and no webbed neck.",
3:"47,XXY is Klinefelter syndrome: a tall male with small testes and gynecomastia.",
4:"Trisomy 21 is associated with atrioventricular septal (endocardial cushion) defects, not coarctation with a webbed neck and absent puberty."},
eli5="The scan shows a pinched spot in the body's main artery, so the arms get high pressure and the legs get weak pulses. Together with short stature and no puberty, that points to Turner syndrome, where a girl has only one X chromosome."),

dict(img='Pring', cap='Barium esophagram, frontal view',
stem="A 7-month-old girl has had noisy breathing since birth, initially attributed to tracheomalacia. Over the past month she has had repeated choking episodes during feeds and two episodes of pneumonia. She has no wheezing, and there is no history of reflux. Inspiratory stridor is heard. A barium swallow is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to confirm the diagnosis?",
choices=["Computed tomography angiogram","Flexible bronchoscopy","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy","24-hour esophageal pH monitoring","Repeat chest x-ray in 3 months"], key=0,
exp="Stridor (tracheal compression) plus choking and feeding trouble (esophageal compression) in an infant, with a barium column <b>indented from the side</b> instead of running straight, suggests a <b>vascular ring</b>, such as a double aortic arch encircling both the trachea and esophagus. The lecture's case followed exactly this sequence: chest x-ray (a paratracheal density) → barium swallow (suggests it) → <b>CT angiogram (confirms it)</b> and defines the vessels for the surgeon. Treatment is cardiothoracic surgery. Incidence is about 7 per 10,000.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A vascular ring compresses the trachea and esophagus (stridor, choking, dysphagia). Barium swallow suggests it, and CT angiography confirms it before surgery.",
wrong={1:"Bronchoscopy can show pulsatile tracheal compression but can't map the vessels that cause it.",
2:"Endoscopy looks at mucosa (reflux, strictures). The problem here is compression from outside.",
3:"pH monitoring tests for reflux. She has no reflux history, and reflux doesn't indent the barium column.",
4:"Recurrent choking and pneumonias aren't benign tracheomalacia. Waiting delays a surgically correctable diagnosis."},
eli5="An extra loop of blood vessel is wrapped around the baby's windpipe and food pipe like a ring, squeezing both. The barium picture shows the dent. A CT scan of the blood vessels shows exactly what to cut."),

dict(img='Aapdamur', cap='Phonocardiogram with simultaneous electrocardiogram',
stem="A girl born at 28 weeks' gestation is now 2 weeks old in the neonatal intensive care unit. Over the past 2 days she has needed more ventilator support. Her pulses are bounding and her pulse pressure is wide. A recording of her heart sounds is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?",
choices=["Indomethacin","Alprostadil","Digoxin","Nitric oxide","Propranolol"], key=0,
exp="The phonocardiogram shows a <b>continuous \"machinery\" murmur</b> through systole and diastole, peaking around S2. In a preterm infant with bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure, that's a <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b>. The ductus normally closes within 6–24 hours but persists more often in <b>preterm infants and neonatal RDS</b> (respiratory distress syndrome). After birth, aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure, so blood runs aorta → pulmonary artery. The lungs are overloaded (hence the rising ventilator needs), and diastolic runoff widens the pulse pressure. Treatment per the lecture: <b>IV indomethacin</b>, catheter closure or surgical ligation. Indomethacin, an NSAID, blocks the prostaglandin E2 that keeps the ductus open.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A persistent PDA in a preterm infant (continuous machinery murmur, bounding pulses) is closed with an NSAID such as indomethacin, which lowers prostaglandin E2.",
wrong={1:"Alprostadil (PGE1) <b>keeps</b> the ductus open. It's for ductal-dependent lesions, the opposite goal.",
2:"Digoxin is an inotrope. It doesn't close the ductus or stop the shunt.",
3:"Nitric oxide treats pulmonary hypertension of the newborn. Lowering pulmonary pressure would increase the left-to-right shunt.",
4:"Propranolol prevents tet spells and has no role in closing a ductus."},
eli5="Before birth, a little tube lets blood skip the lungs. In a premature baby it can stay open, sending too much blood to the lungs. Indomethacin removes the chemical signal that keeps the tube open, so it closes."),

dict(img='Rbasd', cap='Autopsy specimen, right atrium opened (the defect is marked with a diamond)',
stem="A 41-year-old woman had a soft systolic murmur at the left upper sternal border throughout childhood that was never evaluated. In her 30s she developed exertional dyspnea, and over the past year her lips and fingertips became blue and her nails clubbed. She dies of right heart failure. Her heart at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the cyanosis late in her disease?",
choices=["Pulmonary hypertension reversing the shunt","Left ventricular failure with pulmonary edema","Spontaneous narrowing of the septal defect","Recurrent emboli occluding pulmonary arteries","Rupture of the interventricular septum"], key=0,
exp="The specimen shows a large hole in the atrial septum at the fossa ovalis: an <b>atrial septal defect</b>. For decades blood went <b>left atrium → right atrium</b> (high to low pressure), so she was pink. The lecture lists the consequences of a large ASD: right ventricular volume overload with a <b>fixed split S2</b>, atrial arrhythmias and thrombotic stroke. Board add-on: years of excess pulmonary flow remodel the pulmonary arterioles. <b>Pulmonary hypertension</b> eventually pushes right-sided pressure above left, and the shunt <b>reverses</b> (right → left). Deoxygenated blood then enters the systemic circulation, giving cyanosis and clubbing. This is <b>Eisenmenger syndrome</b>, and at that point the defect can no longer be closed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> An uncorrected left-to-right shunt (ASD, VSD, PDA) can cause pulmonary hypertension that reverses the shunt (Eisenmenger syndrome), producing late cyanosis and clubbing.",
wrong={1:"Left heart failure causes dyspnea and pulmonary edema, but not cyanosis with clubbing from mixing. She died of <b>right</b> heart failure.",
2:"A defect that narrowed would reduce shunting and couldn't create right-to-left flow.",
3:"Pulmonary emboli raise pulmonary pressure, but the decades-long left-to-right shunt is what remodels the vessels here.",
4:"The defect is in the atrial septum, and septal rupture is a complication of myocardial infarction, not this course."},
eli5="For years extra blood leaked from the left side to the right side and flooded the lungs. The lung vessels thickened and the pressure on the right side climbed until the leak ran backward. Then blue blood went to the body."),

dict(img='Pvsd', cap='Four-chamber echocardiogram (a label has been covered)',
stem="A 4-month-old boy is referred for a harsh grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border, first heard in the newborn nursery. He is feeding well and gaining weight. An echocardiogram shows a small defect in the muscular ventricular septum (view shown).{FIG}Which of the following is most likely to happen to the murmur as the defect gets smaller over the next year?",
choices=["It grows louder and higher-pitched, then disappears","It becomes softer and lower-pitched as flow falls off","It becomes continuous through systole and diastole","It gains a mid-diastolic rumble at the apex","It does not change until surgical closure"], key=0,
exp="Dr. Baker's point: <b>you can't judge VSD size by loudness</b>. A <b>small VSD is high-pitched and often louder</b>, because a small hole makes a fast, turbulent jet. A <b>large VSD is lower-pitched and often softer</b>, because more blood flows more easily. As a VSD starts to close, the jet speeds up, so the murmur gets <b>louder and higher-pitched</b>. That is a <b>good</b> sign: the defect is closing. When it closes the murmur disappears. Small VSDs (especially muscular ones) often close on their own within the first 2 years. The cardiologist follows with echo to measure size and pressure gradients.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Small VSDs make loud, high-pitched holosystolic murmurs that may get louder as the defect closes. Loudness doesn't indicate a large defect.",
wrong={1:"Softer and lower-pitched describes a <b>large</b> VSD with high flow, not a shrinking one.",
2:"A continuous murmur through systole and diastole is a PDA (or a venous hum), not a VSD.",
3:"An apical mid-diastolic rumble comes from high flow across the mitral valve in a <b>large</b> shunt, not a closing one.",
4:"Small muscular VSDs usually close spontaneously, and the murmur changes as they do."},
eli5="Blowing through a tiny straw makes a louder whistle than blowing through a wide tube. As the hole shrinks, the whistle gets louder and higher, and then it stops when the hole closes."),

dict(img='Ppsvt', cap='12-lead electrocardiogram',
stem="A 6-week-old boy is brought to the emergency department because he has fed poorly and been fussy for 12 hours. His heart rate is 270/min and regular, blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg, capillary refill is 2 seconds, and he is alert. An electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate initial intervention?",
choices=["Ice-water bag applied to the face","Synchronized electrical cardioversion","Intravenous verapamil push","Firm pressure on both eyeballs","Intravenous amiodarone load"], key=0,
exp="A regular, narrow-complex rhythm at 270/min with no visible P waves in an infant is <b>paroxysmal SVT</b>, usually re-entry. Infants tolerate it for hours but then develop poor feeding, irritability and eventually heart failure. This baby is <b>hemodynamically stable</b> (alert, normal BP and perfusion), so start with a <b>vagal manoeuvre</b>. For an infant that means an <b>ice bag over the forehead and nasal bridge for 20–30 seconds</b>, which triggers the <b>diving reflex</b> (vagal tone ↑ → AV-node conduction slows → re-entry breaks). Next is <b>adenosine</b>, a rapid push close to the heart with a flush. <b>Synchronized cardioversion</b> is for an unstable child. Recurrent SVT → ablation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In a stable infant with SVT, start with a vagal manoeuvre such as ice to the face (diving reflex), then adenosine. Cardiovert if the child is unstable.",
wrong={1:"Cardioversion is for an <b>unstable</b> child (hypotensive, poorly perfused, poorly responsive). This infant is stable.",
2:"Board add-on: IV verapamil is avoided in infants because it can cause profound hypotension and cardiovascular collapse.",
3:"The lecture warns <b>not</b> to use ocular pressure: it can cause retinal detachment.",
4:"Amiodarone isn't first-line for stable SVT. Vagal manoeuvres and adenosine come first."},
eli5="The baby's heart is stuck in a fast loop. A cold, wet ice bag on the face sets off a reflex mammals have for diving into cold water. That reflex slows the heart's relay station and can snap the loop."),

dict(img='Lwpw', cap='12-lead electrocardiogram recorded between episodes',
stem="A 15-year-old boy has had several episodes of sudden-onset rapid heartbeat during soccer practice over the past year; each lasts 10–20 minutes and stops abruptly. He fainted once. An electrocardiogram obtained between episodes is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term management?",
choices=["Ablation of the accessory pathway","Lifelong oral digoxin","Daily oral verapamil","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","Reassurance and full sports clearance"], key=0,
exp="Between episodes the ECG shows a <b>short PR interval</b>, a <b>slurred upstroke into the QRS (delta wave)</b> and a <b>widened QRS</b>: ventricular pre-excitation, <b>Wolff-Parkinson-White</b>. An <b>accessory pathway</b> (bundle of Kent) bypasses the AV node and creates a re-entry circuit, giving his paroxysmal tachycardia. WPW peaks in infants and adolescents. Infants show irritability and poor feeding; older children have palpitations, shortness of breath, chest pain and <b>syncope</b>. The lecture: possible antiarrhythmics, but <b>primarily radiofrequency ablation</b>, which cures it by destroying the pathway. Syncope makes this more urgent.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Symptomatic Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome (delta wave, short PR, re-entrant tachycardia) is treated definitively with catheter ablation of the accessory pathway.",
wrong={1:"Board add-on: AV-nodal blockers like digoxin can speed conduction down the accessory pathway during atrial fibrillation and precipitate VF.",
2:"Verapamil is also an AV-nodal blocker and carries the same risk in pre-excitation. It isn't curative.",
3:"An ICD treats ventricular arrhythmias from scar or channelopathies. Ablation removes the cause here.",
4:"He has symptomatic pre-excitation with syncope. That warrants an EP study and ablation, not clearance."},
eli5="This teen was born with an extra electrical wire between the top and bottom of his heart, which lets signals race around in a loop. Doctors can thread a catheter in and burn that one wire, fixing it for good."),

dict(img='Llqt', cap='12-lead electrocardiogram',
stem="A 13-year-old girl fainted at home when her alarm clock rang. She had a similar episode last year when a fire alarm sounded at school. Her paternal uncle died suddenly at age 30. Physical examination is normal. An electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}This patient should avoid which of the following medications?",
choices=["Azithromycin","Amoxicillin","Acetaminophen","Cetirizine","Ibuprofen"], key=0,
exp="The ECG shows a <b>markedly prolonged QT interval</b> with broad T waves. Syncope triggered by a sudden loud sound, plus a family history of sudden death, points to <b>congenital long QT syndrome</b>. The lecture: a genetic ion-channel disorder (about <b>1 in 5,000</b>, ~1,000 US deaths a year) causing unexplained bradycardia, palpitations, <b>syncope, seizure or sudden death</b>. In children a normal QT is under 440 ms and a prolonged one over 460 ms. Long-term treatment is an <b>ICD</b>. She must avoid anything that prolongs the QT further. The lecture case was imipramine; a <b>macrolide such as azithromycin</b> is the classic board example (also fluoroquinolones, ondansetron, antipsychotics). Board add-on: β-blockers are first-line, and an auditory trigger suggests LQT2.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Patients with long QT syndrome must avoid QT-prolonging drugs such as macrolides, which can precipitate torsades de pointes.",
wrong={1:"Amoxicillin doesn't prolong the QT interval.",
2:"Acetaminophen has no effect on cardiac repolarization.",
3:"Cetirizine, a second-generation antihistamine, has negligible QT effect.",
4:"Ibuprofen doesn't prolong the QT interval."},
eli5="Her heart takes too long to reset after each beat, so a sudden scare can tip it into a dangerous rhythm. Some medicines make that reset take even longer, like the antibiotic azithromycin, so she has to avoid them."),

dict(img='Phcm', cap='Apical four-chamber echocardiograms (two views)',
stem="A 16-year-old boy collapsed during a soccer game without contact and was resuscitated with an automated external defibrillator that detected ventricular tachycardia. For 3 months he had noticed palpitations and dizziness after sprints. His electrocardiogram shows large QRS voltages. A bedside echocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following maneuvers would most likely increase the intensity of his systolic murmur?",
choices=["Valsalva maneuver (strain phase)","Squatting from a standing position","Passive elevation of both legs","Sustained isometric handgrip","Moving from standing to lying supine"], key=0,
exp="The echo shows a <b>massively thickened septum and left ventricular wall</b> with a small cavity: <b>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</b>, the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes (36% in the slide's registry). The lecture's exam finding is a <b>harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur that gets louder with Valsalva</b>. The obstruction is dynamic: the less the ventricle fills, the closer the thick septum and mitral leaflet come together. Anything that <b>lowers preload</b> (Valsalva strain, standing up) <b>increases</b> the murmur. Raising preload or afterload (squatting, leg raise, handgrip, lying down) decreases it. HCM is autosomal dominant, more common in males, and treated with an ICD.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The HCM murmur gets louder with maneuvers that reduce left ventricular filling (Valsalva, standing) and softer with those that increase it (squatting, handgrip, leg raise).",
wrong={1:"Squatting raises venous return and afterload, so the ventricle fills more and the murmur <b>softens</b>.",
2:"Passive leg raising increases preload, which <b>decreases</b> the obstruction and the murmur.",
3:"Handgrip increases afterload, which keeps the ventricle fuller and <b>softens</b> the HCM murmur.",
4:"Lying down increases venous return and filling, which <b>softens</b> the murmur."},
eli5="In HCM the heart wall is so thick that when the chamber is nearly empty, the wall almost blocks the exit. Straining like you're on the toilet empties the chamber, so the block gets tighter and the murmur gets louder."),

dict(img='Aasinarr', cap='12-lead electrocardiogram',
stem="During a well-child visit, a 7-year-old girl's pulse feels irregular. She has no symptoms, plays soccer without difficulty, and has no family history of heart disease. Her pulse speeds up when she breathes in and slows when she breathes out. An electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
choices=["Reassurance that this is normal","24-hour Holter monitoring","Referral for an echocardiogram","A trial of low-dose propranolol","Restriction from competitive sports"], key=0,
exp="Every QRS has an identical P wave in front of it with a normal PR interval. Only the spacing changes, gradually and in time with breathing. That is <b>sinus arrhythmia</b>, which the lecture calls the <b>most common \"arrhythmia\" in children</b> and <b>totally normal</b>. The rate rises on inspiration and slows on expiration. Board add-on: the mechanism is respiratory variation in vagal tone. No testing or treatment is needed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Sinus arrhythmia (rate varying with respiration, identical P waves before every QRS) is a normal finding in children and needs only reassurance.",
wrong={1:"A Holter monitor captures intermittent symptomatic arrhythmias. This asymptomatic, breathing-linked irregularity has already been explained.",
2:"There's no murmur or structural concern, so an echo would find nothing.",
3:"There's nothing to treat. A β-blocker would only cause side effects.",
4:"Sinus arrhythmia carries no risk with exercise."},
eli5="When she breathes in, her heart speeds up a little, and when she breathes out, it slows a little. That's how healthy kids' hearts behave, so there's nothing to fix."),

dict(img='Aatriplet', cap='Rhythm strip recorded during symptoms',
stem="A 15-year-old boy reports episodes of \"skipped beats\" and lightheadedness while playing basketball. He drinks two energy drinks most days. Examination is normal. A rhythm strip recorded during symptoms is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
choices=["Cardiology referral and evaluation","Reassurance that the beats are benign","Stop energy drinks and recheck next year","Urgent synchronized cardioversion","Daily oral verapamil"], key=0,
exp="The strip shows <b>runs of three consecutive wide, bizarre QRS complexes</b> without preceding P waves, and they recur. The lecture's counting rule: one PVC is common, <b>2 in a row is a couplet</b>, and <b>3 in a row is ventricular tachycardia</b> (here nonsustained), which is \"a little worrisome\". Add <b>symptoms during exertion</b>, and a referral reason applies: a possible arrhythmia with dizziness. He needs a <b>pediatric cardiology evaluation</b>: Holter or event monitor, echocardiogram (for HCM and other structural disease) and exercise testing. Cutting caffeine is sensible but not sufficient.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Isolated uniform PVCs are usually benign, but runs of three or more (nonsustained VT), especially with exertional symptoms, need cardiology evaluation.",
wrong={1:"Isolated uniform PVCs can be reassured. Runs of three with symptoms on exertion cannot.",
2:"Energy drinks provoke ectopy, but exertional nonsustained VT needs evaluation now, not in a year.",
3:"Cardioversion treats sustained, unstable tachycardia. These are brief self-terminating runs.",
4:"Verapamil targets AV-node-dependent rhythms. It's not a treatment for ventricular ectopy, and the cause hasn't been found."},
eli5="An occasional extra beat is like a hiccup, but three wild beats in a row is the start of a dangerous rhythm. When that happens during sports, a heart specialist needs to look for the reason."),

dict(img='Peffcxr', cap='Chest radiograph',
stem="A 6-year-old girl with juvenile idiopathic arthritis had surgical repair of a ventricular septal defect 3 weeks ago. After a mild viral illness she has become pale and increasingly short of breath. Blood pressure is 78/56 mm Hg and falls by 14 mm Hg on inspiration; her heart sounds are muffled and her neck veins are distended. A chest x-ray is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Pericardiocentesis","Intravenous furosemide","Intravenous dobutamine","High-dose ibuprofen","Intravenous antibiotics"], key=0,
exp="The x-ray shows a <b>massively enlarged, globular (\"water bottle\") cardiac silhouette</b>. With <b>hypotension, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds</b> (Beck triad) and a <b>pulsus paradoxus</b> over 10 mm Hg, this is a large <b>pericardial effusion with tamponade</b>. Weeks after heart surgery it's typical of post-pericardiotomy syndrome (board add-on). Fluid in the sac stops the heart filling. The lecture's treatment for a symptomatic effusion is <b>pericardiocentesis</b>: a needle under the subcostal angle (subxiphoid) to drain the fluid. The before-and-after films were \"life-saving\".<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Cardiac tamponade (hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds, pulsus paradoxus, enlarged globular heart) requires urgent pericardiocentesis.",
wrong={1:"A diuretic lowers preload. A heart compressed by fluid depends on high filling pressure, so furosemide would worsen the hypotension.",
2:"An inotrope can't overcome mechanical compression. Removing the fluid is the treatment.",
3:"NSAIDs treat pericardial inflammation, but tamponade needs the fluid drained now.",
4:"There's no sign of purulent pericarditis. Antibiotics don't relieve compression."},
eli5="A big pocket of fluid has built up around her heart, squeezing it so it can't fill. Drawing the fluid out with a needle lets the heart fill and pump again."),

dict(img='Aajaneway', cap="Photograph of the patient's palm",
stem="A 17-year-old emancipated minor who lives in a shelter comes to the emergency department with a temperature of 40°C (104°F), rigors, and chills. He has needle track marks on both forearms. A grade 3/6 harsh holosystolic murmur is heard along the left sternal border. The lesions on his palm are not tender. A photograph is shown.{FIG}These skin lesions are most likely caused by which of the following?",
choices=["Septic microemboli from a valve vegetation","Immune complex deposition in small vessels","Thrombocytopenia from bone marrow failure","Leukocytoclastic vasculitis of skin vessels","Allergic reaction to injected adulterants"], key=0,
exp="Fever, rigors, a new harsh murmur and injection drug use mean <b>infective endocarditis</b>, the lecture's final case. He also had splinter hemorrhages, Roth spots and a mitral vegetation on echo. The photograph shows <b>Janeway lesions</b>: <b>flat, painless</b>, erythematous macules on the palms and soles caused by <b>septic microemboli</b> from the vegetation. Contrast <b>Osler nodes</b>: raised and <b>painful</b> (\"Osler = ouch\"), caused by <b>immune complex</b> deposition. Board add-on: in injection drug users the classic answer is tricuspid valve endocarditis with <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>. Get three sets of blood cultures before antibiotics, plus an echocardiogram.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Janeway lesions (painless palmar or plantar macules) come from septic emboli; Osler nodes (painful) come from immune complexes. Both are signs of infective endocarditis.",
wrong={1:"Immune complex deposition produces <b>Osler nodes</b>: raised, painful nodules on the finger pads.",
2:"Thrombocytopenic purpura gives petechiae and bruising, not palmar macules with fever and a murmur.",
3:"Leukocytoclastic vasculitis gives palpable purpura, usually on the legs.",
4:"Drug reactions cause urticaria or rash, not lesions tied to fever, rigors and a new murmur."},
eli5="Germs growing on a heart valve break off in tiny clumps that lodge in small vessels of the hands. That leaves painless red spots called Janeway lesions."),

dict(img='Aaapb', cap='Rhythm strip',
stem="A 10-year-old boy is noted to have an occasional irregular heartbeat at a sports physical. He feels well and drinks several caffeinated sodas daily. A rhythm strip is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the early beats?",
choices=["Premature atrial contractions","Premature ventricular contractions","Sinus arrhythmia","Second-degree atrioventricular block","Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia"], key=0,
exp="The early beats have a <b>narrow QRS</b> preceded by a <b>P wave of a different shape</b>: <b>premature atrial contractions</b>. The lecture: these ectopic beats start in the atrium but not the SA node, so the <b>P-wave contour, PR interval and timing differ</b> from a normal sinus beat. Occasional PACs and PVCs are common in healthy children and are often brought on by <b>caffeine</b> (\"Did you stop at Starbucks today?\"). They're perceived as palpitations or \"skipped beats\". They need no treatment beyond cutting stimulants.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A premature atrial contraction is an early narrow beat with an abnormal P wave. In children it is usually benign and often caffeine-related.",
wrong={1:"PVCs are <b>wide and bizarre with no preceding P wave</b>. These early beats are narrow and have a P wave.",
2:"In sinus arrhythmia every P wave looks identical and the rate changes gradually with breathing. There are no abrupt early beats.",
3:"In second-degree AV block a P wave fails to conduct (a dropped QRS). Here an extra early beat appears.",
4:"PSVT is a sustained run at 180–280/min, not single early beats."},
eli5="Every so often a different spot in the upper heart fires a beat a little early. It has a slightly different 'signature' wave and is harmless, often from too much caffeine."),

dict(img='Pbav', cap='Intraoperative photograph of the aortic valve',
stem="A 24-year-old man undergoes aortic valve surgery for severe stenosis. A systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border with an ejection click had been noted since he was 3 years old. His father and sister have the same valve abnormality. The valve is shown at surgery.{FIG}In addition to valve dysfunction, this condition most increases his long-term risk of which of the following?",
choices=["Aortic aneurysm and dissection","Mitral valve prolapse","Coronary artery aneurysm formation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary vein stenosis"], key=0,
exp="A thickened valve with <b>two leaflets</b> and a narrowed orifice, a right-upper-sternal-border ejection murmur with a click from early childhood, and affected relatives: <b>bicuspid aortic valve</b>. The lecture: an <b>inherited</b> condition with 2 leaflets instead of 3, in <b>1–2%</b> of people, males to females 2:1. Over time it brings aortic stenosis and regurgitation, an <b>increased risk of aortic aneurysm and aortic dissection</b>, and <b>nearly all patients need valve surgery</b> in their lifetime. Children are followed with echo about every 2 years, and intervention is timed to pressure-gradient criteria (\"if it ain't broke, don't fix it\").<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A bicuspid aortic valve causes stenosis and regurgitation and an associated aortopathy, with a long-term risk of aortic aneurysm and dissection.",
wrong={1:"Mitral valve prolapse is a separate myxomatous valve disease, not a consequence of a bicuspid aortic valve.",
2:"Coronary artery aneurysms are the classic complication of Kawasaki disease.",
3:"Pressure overload from stenosis causes concentric LV hypertrophy, but that isn't the genetic sarcomere disease HCM.",
4:"Pulmonary vein stenosis is unrelated to bicuspid aortic valve disease."},
eli5="He was born with an aortic valve that has two flaps instead of three. Besides the valve wearing out, the wall of the big artery above it is weaker, so it can balloon out or tear."),
]
